    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_6809437f1ba69d7db4443514e9d82af25e81ccec2d92a5f51ab4918f6ac55dae",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The zero-counting formula supplies a representation bridge from the analytically tractable argument function S to the endpoint-convention count N, which half-weights zeros at the stated endpoints. In the relative count, shared smooth growth cancels under the standing comparison, and a short increment localizes the remaining fluctuation to the relative zero population of a short interval; Lemma 1 then yields both positive and negative excursions on sets of positive measure. This gains access to discrete zero behavior through moment estimates for S, but loses exact equality because the source says only 'essentially.' The proof burden is to control the omitted error and normalization terms, verify the hypotheses of Lemma 1, and recover the damaged thresholds, powers, shifts, and measure bounds; the corollary alone does not yet select a particular empty zero interval.",
