    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_75d9fe872af2149ae395cdc3365124d96e55758ea83153c2cfab86111a1ccaa9",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For Lemma 2 the coefficient is taken to be the direct character difference. Mertens's theorem is then said to give the displayed prime sum a leading 2 log log scale, with an OCR-unsafe lower-order term, after which the conclusion follows by the same method as above. This supports a modular reading: the coefficient-derived prime-sum input changes while the preceding inferential machinery is reused. The gain is to isolate the replaceable analytic input; the loss is that this span does not preserve the sum, cutoff, variables, error term, or parameter restriction well enough for quantitative reuse. The remaining proof burden lies in the earlier approximation and moment argument and in verifying that the stated assumption on q permits the unreadable parameter choice.",
